{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>To the root of a sojourner's family.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): a gentile who never converted, and he is of the family of the sojourner (ger). And the meaning of the verse is: whether he is sold to a sojourner (ger) who is a settler among us, or to the root, that is, to the gentile himself — and so the Targum renders לארמאי — in any case, after he is sold, redemption shall be his. For the matter is open to any one of Israel, and that is what it says: one of his brothers may redeem him; or the relatives who take precedence, and this is: or his uncle, or his uncle's son, may redeem him; or he may redeem himself — this is: or his hand attains and he is redeemed." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'to the root' — this is Rome, and it called it 'root' because the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to uproot it, as it is said (Daniel 7:11–12): ״והובד גשמה ויהיבת ליקדת אשא״. And this is what they said in the Midrash: 'And if the hand of a sojourner or settler attains' — the sojourner, this is Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon; and 'settler,' this is the kingdom of Media; 'and is sold to a sojourner, a settler among you' — this is the kingdom of Greece; 'or to the root of a sojourner's family' — this is Rome. The Midrash drew out from this verse an allusion to the four kingdoms, and in my opinion they did this with complete intention according to the subject of the parashah, for there is in the parashah an arousal and an allusion and a promise to Israel regarding the redemption of the subjugation of the four kingdoms. And also there is in it a further allusion that the redemption depends on repentance, yet even if they do not repent, it is impossible to delay beyond the appointed time of the end; and when the appointed time of the end arrives, no servitude will remain for Israel except the service of HaShem, blessed be He. And there is in it a further allusion that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to bring the gentiles to account for having subjugated Israel, and this is from the time of the exile until the year of the future redemption, which the verse calls Yovel (Jubilee). And behold, the entire parashah in its content testifies to this, and because of this the Sages, of blessed memory, took an allusion and a sign from the verse for the four kingdoms, and attached to the fourth kingdom, which it called 'root,' 'after he is sold, redemption shall be his.' And it is known that the term 'after' (אחרי) is distant and remote, alluding to this last exile." "<b>And it says</b> 'one of his brothers may redeem him' — this is Mashiach ben David, who is from the tribe of Judah, the most distinguished among all the tribes. And further, 'one of his brothers' includes the meaning that the redeemer will be flesh and blood like the rest of the brothers, born of a father and mother in the likeness of Moses, who was the first redeemer in Egypt. And it says 'or his hand attains and he is redeemed' — that is, with money, and this is the redemption through repentance, which is likened to silver, for just as silver is white and pure, so repentance purifies the soul and whitens sins. And it says 'and he shall reckon with his purchaser' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, who acquires heaven and earth — that He will bring to account every nation that subjugated Israel, 'from the year of his sale to him,' that is, from the time they began to subjugate them until the year of the time of redemption. And He called the redemption 'the year of Yovel (Jubilee)' because Yovel is a time of freedom, as it is said (Leviticus 25:10): ״וקראתם דרור בארץ לכל יושביה יובל היא״, and further, after the manner of (Psalms 76:12): ״יובילו שי למורא״, and after the manner of what is written (Isaiah 66:20): ״והביאו את כל אחיכם מכל הגוים מנחה לה׳״. And it says 'if there are still many years' and 'if little remains in the years,' that is, that the redemption will be according to the years, for according to the greatness of the repentance, the redemption will draw near or recede. And it says 'and if he is not redeemed by these' — that is, if he is not redeemed through repentance during these years of exile — 'then he shall go out in the year of Yovel, he and his sons with him,' that is, the time of the redemption will not be delayed beyond the appointed end. And this is the opinion of Rabbi Yehoshua, who said: whether they repent or whether they do not repent, they are immediately redeemed, as it is said (Isaiah 60:22): ״אני ה׳ בעתה אחישנה״ — if they merit, 'I will hasten it'; if they do not merit, 'at the appointed time.' And it says 'he and his sons with him,' alluding to the two future gatherings, which are the exiled and the scattered who are with them; this is what it says: 'he and his sons with him.' And this is what is attached to it: 'for the children of Israel are servants to Me' — for when the appointed time of redemption arrives, they are not delayed even one hour, and they will be servants to none but the Holy One, blessed be He. And it says 'they are My servants, whom I brought out of the land of Egypt,' to liken this redemption to the redemption from Egypt, for once the end arrived, they immediately went out. 'I am HaShem your Elohim,' who promises you this for the future."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:42.059667352Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}